In article <15609@wpi.UUCP>, cor@wpi.UUCP (Corydon T Shimer) writes:
> I just got a copy of nethack 3.0, which I downloaded from upenn.
> I installed it on my 'compatible' XT now, it doesn't print the screen right.
> It prints escape sequences on my screen
> instead of having them execute... the escapes look like
> arrows pointing left... plus all the usual garbage i.e.   [7;8H   or whatever.
> The program runs correct, but the screen output is a jumble.
> I installed it on another persons PC and it worked fine.

There are several ways to drive a screen on a PC, including writing
to the hardware directly, using the BIOS, and using ANSI.SYS.
ANSI uses escape sequences like ESCAPE [7;8H, so what's presumably
happening is that hack expects ANSI.SYS and your PC isn't set up to
use it.  You need to change your CONFIG.SYS to tell it to do so -
check out the CONFIG.SYS on teh PC where it worked for details.
And read teh documentation that comes with nethack - you may need
NANSI.SYS or one of the other "improved" versions of ANSI.SYS?
